    "reasoning": "All 16 answers agree with their own captured worked solutions. q-211462: (x,y) maps to (x/2,3-y), so the minimum (2,3) becomes the maximum point (1,0); the maximum value is 0. q-3946: the positive quadratic leading term grows without bound. q-99300: the expression grows like t^8. q-1952 and q-48565: differentiating a^u gives a^u ln(a) times u', producing factors 9 and 4x respectively; the selected forms are equivalent to the solutions despite omitted multiplication dots. q-48515: the product-rule derivative is 3 ln(x)+(3x-2)/x, which equals 1 at x=1. q-34325: -450 degrees is coterminal with 270 degrees, placing the terminal ray on the negative y-axis, between quadrants III and IV. q-103812: cosecant has range (-infinity,-1] union [1,infinity), no global maximum, and excludes integer multiples of pi from its domain, so all statements are false. q-65307: its domain excludes integer multiples of pi, its asymptotes occur there, and it has no y-intercept; only III is true. q-103818 and q-66667: the shifts are integer multiples of cosecant's period 2pi, giving 15 and 5sqrt(2)-1. q-158071 and q-158065: periodicity and oddness give -11/3 and -11/4. q-47039: the left and right limits are -infinity and +infinity, so the two-sided limit does not exist. q-67609: continuity permits substitution, giving 1/sin(2pi/3)=2sqrt(3)/3. q-31338: the chain rule gives 3csc^2(2pi-3x), which equals 3 at pi/2. No earlier submitted answers or grades are supplied; confirmation rests on the captured worked solutions.",
